-- A WITH inside a rule reaches only what the rule it calls reads in its own body
-- and through the rules that one calls. It does not reach an input that only a
-- default reads: `b`'s default reads `r`, and a root works `b` out from the root's
-- own `r` (R8 rule 3), so a WITH on `r` written inside `h` would go nowhere. It is
-- refused, as it was before defaults could read inputs (TYPICALLY-ONE-BEHAVIOUR-SPEC.md
-- W7, §4.3, silent review S4).

§ `S`
    GIVEN r IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY 2
          b IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY (r PLUS 1)

-- `g` names `b` and not `r`
GIVETH A NUMBER
g MEANS b TIMES 10

-- ERROR: nothing in `g` reads `r` itself, so the value has nowhere to go
GIVETH A NUMBER
h MEANS g WITH r IS 100

-- Fine, and the control: at a #EVAL the same WITH reaches `b`'s default, because
-- a root works it out from its own values
#EVAL g WITH r IS 100

-- Fine: a rule that reads `r` itself takes a WITH on it
GIVETH A NUMBER
`g and r` MEANS b PLUS r

GIVETH A NUMBER
`h and r` MEANS `g and r` WITH r IS 100
